# Hostinger (LiteSpeed) server config. Ships to the docroot via public/ ->
# .output/public -> dist-static -> FTP. Keep this file minimal: a syntax error
# here returns 500 for EVERY request on the site, and this business has one
# weekly revenue window that closes Friday 18:00 Berlin.
#
# Deliberately NOT here, and each omission is load-bearing:
#   - No HTTPS-force rule. Hostinger already 301s http -> https at the vhost
#     level (verified). Behind their TLS terminator %{HTTPS} can read "off" on
#     a real HTTPS request, which is the classic way to infinite-loop a site
#     on this host.
#   - No "Options -Indexes". AllowOverride may forbid Options entirely -> 500.
#     Every directory in dist-static already contains an index.html anyway.
#   - No SPA catch-all rewrite to /index.html. It would turn every 404 into a
#     200 and defeat the ErrorDocument below.
#   - No trailing-slash manipulation. LiteSpeed's DirectorySlash already 301s
#     /about -> /about/; the real fix is emitting the slash-terminated form in
#     canonicals and the sitemap, which src/lib/seo.ts does.

# The branded 404 (dist-static/404.html, generated by scripts/build-static.mjs
# from the app's own NotFoundComponent). Must be a LOCAL PATH, never a full
# URL - "ErrorDocument 404 https://..." turns the response into a 302 and
# loses the 404 status entirely.
ErrorDocument 404 /404.html

# The FTP deploy action writes its sync manifest into the docroot, where it was
# publicly fetchable and published a complete inventory of every file on the
# server. It has to stay on disk (deleting it forces a full resync), so deny it
# over HTTP instead.
<Files ".ftp-deploy-sync-state.json">
  Require all denied
</Files>

<IfModule mod_rewrite.c>
  RewriteEngine On

  # www -> apex, 301. Both hostnames served identical content with no redirect,
  # so Google was seeing two duplicate sites.
  RewriteCond %{HTTP_HOST} ^www\.dhakakacchi\.com$ [NC]
  RewriteRule ^(.*)$ https://dhakakacchi.com/$1 [R=301,L]
</IfModule>
